          %title Feynman diagrams
          %h2#FeynmanDiagrams Feynman diagrams
          %p
            So far we considered only the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            on a consistent perturbative S-matrix /
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+products"} time-ordered products
            and its formal consequences. Now we discuss the actual construction of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+products"} time-ordered products
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
          %p
            We first discuss how
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+product"} time-ordered product
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#PerturbativeSMatrixAndTimeOrderedProducts"} above
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TimeOrderedProductAwayFromDiagonal"}
            below). Moreover, we discuss how on that locus the time-ordered product is naturally expressed as a sum of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/products+of+distributions"} products of distributions
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+propagators"} Feynman propagators
            that are labeled by
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+perturbation+series"} Feynman perturbation series
            (prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FeynmanPerturbationSeriesAwayFromCoincidingPoints"}
            below).
          %p
            This means that the full
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-ordered+product"} time-ordered product
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extension+of+distributions"} extension of distributions
            of these
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"}> scattering amplitudes
              \- to the locus of coinciding vertices. The space of possible such extensions turns out to be finite-dimensional in each order of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi ℏ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi j
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi ℏ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g/\hbar, j/\hbar
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point-supported+distributions"} point-supported distributions
              at the interaction points whose
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/degree+of+a+distribution"} scaling degree
              is bounded by the given Feynman propagators.
          #TuplesOfCompactlySupportedPolynomialLocalFunctionalsWithPairwiseDisjointSupport.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              For
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \in \mathbb{N}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %msub
                          %mi ℱ
                          %mi loc
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                        %mi g
                        %mo ,
                        %mi j
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                        %mo )
                      %mi pds
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi k
                    %mo ↪
                    %msup
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %msub
                          %mi ℱ
                          %mi loc
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                        %mi g
                        %mo ,
                        %mi j
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                        %mo )
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\left(\mathcal{F}_{loc}\langle g,j\rangle\right)^{\otimes^k}_{pds}
                    \\hookrightarrow
                    \\left(\mathcal{F}_{loc}\langle g,j\rangle\right)^{\otimes^k}
            %p
              for the subspace of the
              = succeed "-fold" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
              of the space of compactly supported polynomial local densities (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CompactlySupportedPolynomialLocalDensities"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tuples"} tuples
              which have pairwise disjoint spacetime
